/*
  ============================================================================
   VESSEL MEASUREMENT RECORDER (VMR)  (v6.5; was Vessel Motion Recorder)
   Ship motion, stability, waves, position, location marks / CTD casts and
   weather, logged to microSD with a GPS-disciplined clock; OLED screens and a
   Wi-Fi hotspot dashboard.

   Written by Aspect Wave LLC (Sophie Scopazzi) and Claude (Anthropic),
   2026.
  ============================================================================

  HARDWARE
    - Adafruit ESP32-S2 Feather w/ w.FL antenna (MAX17048 fuel gauge @ 0x36)
    - Adalogger FeatherWing (PCF8523 RTC @ 0x68, microSD CS = pin 10)
    - Ultimate GPS FeatherWing (PA1616D) on Serial1 (RX/TX pins), 9600 baud
    - FeatherWing OLED 128x64 (SH1107 @ 0x3C), buttons A=9, B=6, C=5
    - 24LC32 I2C EEPROM breakout (0x50) - STEMMA QT
    - BNO085 9-DOF breakout (0x4A) - STEMMA QT
    - The controls, by default on the I2C boards (v8.1; card 17 changes it):
        PCF8574 (0x20): P0 pot switch, P1 screen button, P2 Mark, P3 Cast
          (each pin -> button / switch -> GND; switch closed = display ON)
        ADS7830 (0x48): channel 0 = the 10K linear pot's wiper (ends to
          VIN and GND)
      or on the Feather itself (as built before v8.1): screen button ->
      pin 12 and GND; pot ends -> 3V and GND, wiper -> A5 (GPIO 8); pot
      switch -> pin 11 and GND
    - Latching power switch between battery and Feather (hardware only)
    - Indicator LEDs (v8.1; pins set in card 17, default Mark A0, Recording
      A1, Fault A2): Feather pin -> 1-2.2 kOhm -> LED -> GND, one pin each
    - Wind meter (v8.2, optional, card 16): Calypso ULP RS485 (NMEA 0183) via a
      SparkFun SP3485 breakout at 3.3 V: TX-O -> pin 12, RX-I -> pin 11,
      RTS -> A5; ULP green -> A, yellow -> B, brown -> 3V, white -> GND.
      Those three Feather pins are then not controls (card 17).
      Card 16 (v8.3): Stream (default) or asked at 0.5-4 Hz ($ULPI*00; only
      units bought as weather stations, or re-set to on-demand with Calypso's
      PC configurator), the north mark's offset from the bow, its height above
      the keel and from the centreline (with the draft: motion correction).

    A5 is used for the pot on purpose: it is on ADC1. A0-A4 are on ADC2,
    which does not read reliably while Wi-Fi is running on the ESP32 (as
    outputs, A0-A3 drive the LEDs without trouble).

    I2C add-ons (card 16), on the STEMMA QT chain - the PCF8574 and ADS7830
    are on by default (the controls), the others optional; the address is
    set by the jumpers on each board (pick the same one in card 16):
    - BMP388 pressure, SHT30 air temperature / humidity
    - PCF8574 GPIO expander (0x20-0x27): buttons from a pin to GND (internal
      weak pull-ups): screen button, pot switch, Mark, Cast (which pin does
      what: card 17). Its INT pin ->
      Feather A4 lets these buttons wake the VMR from Datalogger sleep
      (INT is open drain; A4's internal pull-up holds it high).
    - ADS7830 8-channel ADC (0x48, 0x49 or 0x4B - not 0x4A, the IMU): the
      pot can move to a channel (wiper to the channel, ends on VIN and GND).
    GPS antenna: the dashboard shows "external" only for an active antenna
    (it draws current); a passive one or a bad cable shows as internal.
    The GPS wing's red LED blinks every 1 s while searching, every 15 s
    with a fix.

  ARDUINO IDE SETUP
    Board:  "Adafruit Feather ESP32-S2"  (esp32 by Espressif core)
    Partition Scheme: "Minimal SPIFFS (1.9MB APP with OTA/128KB SPIFFS)"
      for firmware updates over Wi-Fi (card 19). The default "TinyUF2 No
      OTA" also runs everything else, but has no slot for an update.
    Libraries (Library Manager):
      Adafruit SH110X        (pulls in Adafruit GFX + Adafruit BusIO)
      RTClib                 (Adafruit)
      Adafruit GPS Library
      Adafruit BNO08x
      Adafruit MAX1704X
      Adafruit BMP3XX Library, Adafruit SHT31 Library, Adafruit PCF8574,
      Adafruit ADS7830       (the I2C add-ons, card 16; pull in Adafruit
                              Unified Sensor)
    SD, WiFi, WebServer are part of the ESP32 core.
    Firmware update over Wi-Fi (card 19): Sketch > Export Compiled Binary,
    then upload VMR.ino.bin from the sketch's build folder (not the
    .merged, .bootloader or .partitions file).
    No EEPROM library needed - the 24LC32 is driven directly over Wire.

  CONTROLS
    Screen button (pin 12) or OLED button B ... next screen
      (while paused, B resumes)
    Screen 2 (roll graph, length set on the dashboard: 30 s / 1 / 2 / 5 / 10 min):
      Button A ................................ graph TOP (starboard) up 5 deg (wraps)
      Button C ................................ graph BOTTOM (port) down 5 deg (wraps)
    Screen 3 (position):
      Hold A + C for 3 s ...................... toggle level-zero (tare)
    Screen 4 (all-time maxima):
      Hold A + C for 3 s ...................... reset all-time maxima
    Screen 5 (GPS): display only
    Screen 6 (clinometer):
      Hold A + C for 3 s ...................... reset the drag pointers
    Screen 10 (G-meter):
      Hold A + C for 3 s ...................... reset the peaks
    Pot ....................................... OLED brightness
    Pot switch ................................ OLED on/off
    The dashboard can also pick the screen, brightness and on/off. The
    brightness comes from the knob OR the dashboard slider, chosen with the
    checkbox in card 3 (kept in EEPROM); the switch acts only when flipped.
    Recording to the microSD card is started/stopped from the dashboard.

  BOOT: the Adafruit logo, then the Aspect Wave LLC logo, 2 s each on every
    boot (power-on, after a firmware update, any restart), then the boot
    list: "Vessel Measurement / Recorder vX.XX" over a rule, then each part as it
    starts, one every 0.5 s, in two columns (name left, OK / FAIL at the column's
    right edge; past 6 rows the oldest row scrolls off the top), the full list held
    2 s (12 s if anything failed). All at 50 % brightness, even with the OLED
    switched off; the pot and switch apply after. About 12 s from power-on to
    the first screen.

  SCREENS
    1  Local time (ZD) + live roll/pitch/rate of turn + 1-minute max
    2  Roll graph over the chosen length (limits set with A / C or dashboard)
    3  Local + UTC time, lat/lon, averaged SOG / COG, GPS sats used / in
       view and best signal, then health: battery, phones on the Wi-Fi
       (weakest signal), CPU temperature, SD and IMU status
    4  All-time maxima from EEPROM with the date each was set
    5  GPS detail: fix type, sats, HDOP, lat/lon, altitude, SOG, COG (instantaneous), fix age
    6  Clinometer: needle = live roll on a +/-40 deg scale (starboard down
       swings right, as seen looking forward), with drag pointers at the
       largest roll each way since reset (hold A+C, or the dashboard)
    7  Location marks (v6.2, replaces the stern view): "Marks n total", then
       newest first in the boot list's font: number (5C = cast 5), LT hhmm and
       the latitude on the first line, the longitude under it (dd-mm.mmmmH,
       ddd-mm.mmmmH); left of the longitude, one space in: a cast's duration
       and drift (or OPEN; in the serial monitor's small font when the normal
       one would run into the longitude), or - for a mark. No notes here. The Reset I2C bus (and any STEMMA power cycle)
       sets the OLED up again and redraws the screen it was on.
    8  Sky plot: every satellite in view (GSV) by elevation and azimuth,
       north up; filled = used in the fix (GSA), bigger = stronger signal
    9  At the dock: trim in feet-inches (by the stern / head) and list to
       0.01 deg, means over the dock card's window (30 s - 5 min; "45/60s"
       while it fills); "UNCAL" until calibrated from the drafts
    10 G-meter: the felt push in the deck plane (up = forward, right =
       starboard; gravity included, so a starboard heel puts the dot to
       starboard), 0.3 s smoothed, with a short trail, rings every 0.1 g
       (0.2 g once a peak passes 0.3 g), peak-hold ticks (hold A+C to
       reset) and vertical g on the bar (0.5-1.5 g, 1 g = still)
    11 Waves (v7.4): the Waves card's spectra against period (log, 4-30 s),
       the last estimate kept (marked OLD m:ss) while the window refills,
       each scaled to its own peak: roll thick, pitch thin, heave dotted;
       the header has Te (pitch peak), Tr (roll peak) and heave Hs, or,
       black on white, SYNC! (Te within 10 % of Tr) / PARA! (of Tr/2).
       Until there are 8.5 min of steady motion it says how much is in;
       nothing at the dock, paused or with the IMU off
    12 Wave statistics (v7.41): the Waves card's numbers - Te and Tr, Te / Tr
       with SYNC! / PARA! / clear, heave Hs (m) and its peak period, the
       felt roll period (card 1, 10 min) - and the state: header LIVE, or OLD
       m:ss for the last estimate kept; bottom line the window and the next
       update, or the time left until the first estimate, or why there is none
    13 Wi-Fi devices (v7.41): the last 4 phones / laptops on the hotspot, as
       card 18: the name it was given (or the phone type and the end of its
       Wi-Fi address), filled dot = on now with the time it joined (LT),
       hollow = gone with the time it left; the weakest signal of those on
    14 Wind (v8.3; in the cycle only with the wind meter on): true wind, live
       (1-s mean) big, from deg T, a dial (N up; bow up without a heading),
       the 2-min mean and the apparent wind with its 10-min gust; MC = motion
       corrected. Without a GPS fix it shows the apparent wind and says so.

  WI-FI DASHBOARD
    Join network AP_SSID (password AP_PASS), browse to http://192.168.4.1
    Header: local time (big) with ZD selector; UTC, date, battery and CPU
      temperature. ZD uses the nautical sign: UTC = LT + ZD (PDT is ZD +7).
      Until GPS time arrives, an open dashboard sets the clock (and RTC)
      from the phone or laptop on the hotspot ("time from WiFi") and,
      once per boot, ZD from its time zone. GPS takes over as soon as it has a fix.
      Under it: uptime since the last restart (d h:mm:ss).
      CPU temperature is the ESP32-S2's own die sensor: it reads above the
      air, more so with Wi-Fi busy - a check for a box cooking in the sun.
    Top right: Pause (tall) with the ZD selector under it; Dock (the At the
      dock card), Logger (datalogger mode), Charge and the theme (Day /
      Night / Red) beside them.
    Controls: on/off buttons are highlighted when on; settings use < >
    arrows.
    Welcome (v6.5): the first time a phone opens the page, a box with the
      Aspect Wave logo (GET /logo.svg, only then, cached by the phone), the
      name and what the VMR does; Continue closes it; "Don't show this again on this phone"
      keeps it away (per phone); About in the footer shows it again.
    Wi-Fi idle: with no phone on the hotspot for 1 min (from boot, or after
      the last one leaves) the CPU drops to 80 MHz, the slowest Wi-Fi allows;
      everything else carries on (logging, OLED, GPS, IMU, waves) and the
      hotspot stays on at full power. A phone joining brings 240 MHz back
      within half a second, before its page loads. Logger sets
      its own speed.

    Numbered cards, top to bottom:
    1  GPS & motion, values centred (the GPS button in the title row hides the
       position, SOG and COG; per phone): position; SOG and COG averaged over the SOG / COG average
       setting (1-60 s, default 10; set in card 14). COG is the direction of the average velocity, so
       it wraps through 000/360 correctly and near-zero speeds barely
       count; it shows -- below COG_AVG_MIN_KN. The instantaneous SOG / COG, altitude,
       sats and HDOP are in card 14 (SOG and COG labelled, v8.5). Then
       three across, labelled (v8.5): roll (PORT / STBD beside it), rate of
       turn (deg/min) and pitch (a down / up arrow beside it). The Mag
       button in the title row (per phone; Yaw before v8.5) adds a row:
       variation (typed in, E / W, EEPROM), the magnetic heading and the
       deviation = COG - mag - variation over the last minute at 3 kn or
       more on a steady course (COG stands in for the gyro). The field
       strength, dip and calibration are in card 16. Relative yaw is gone
       (v8.5); the ROT deadband still applies but is no longer shown.
       Under them three across, all over card 5's 10 or 30 min window: the
       roll period (median of the rolls in it), the significant roll and
       the largest roll.
       GM estimate (card 5): GM from the natural roll period with the IMO
       formula (shown in feet, metres beside it), GM = (2 C B / T)^2, C = 0.373 +
       0.023 B/d - 0.043 L/100 (IS Code 2008, A 2.3), B, d (the quarter-mean draft;
       mean of fwd and aft without a mid draft)
       and L = LBP (LOA if none) from the ship data (card 15). T is the peak
       of the roll spectrum (the Waves analysis: last 17 min, every 30 s),
       not the roll period above, which mixes in rolling forced at the
       wave period and made GM read high (v7.3; 2026-11-01 loaded: 18-19 ft
       against CargoMax 14.48, the spectrum peak 15.4). No GM for the first
       8.5 min of steady motion (countdown), at the dock or paused. When the
       roll peak is within 10 % of the encounter period Te the GM is still
       given, with a note "Te ≈ Tr: near resonance" (the Waves card flags it
       too). Until v7.32 that blanked it: overnight 2026-11-03 it blanked 81 %
       of the estimates, which were as good as the rest (mean 14.14 against
       14.16 ft; CargoMax 14.48) - the roll peak stayed at 15.3 s while Te
       moved 11.4-15.9 s, so it was the natural roll. A cross-check only. The CSV gm_ft column is the same value
       (in feet; files from before v2.6 have gm_m in metres - Plot from
       file reads either).
    2  Graphs: roll (left axis, starboard up) and pitch (right axis, bow up
       up), 30 s / 1 / 2 / 5 / 10 min. Arrows above and below set each
       axis's top and bottom in 1 deg steps (top: > higher; bottom: > further down), anywhere
       from -70 to +70 deg, e.g. pitch -5 to -3 when trimmed; the top
       always stays above the bottom (default +/-5). The roll limits are
       shared with the OLED graph (A / C buttons step 5).
       5 and 10 min (here and on the OLED) draw an amplitude envelope: the
       highest and lowest angle within a sliding window of at least one
       roll (1.25 x the roll period, 8-30 s; 15 s until one is measured).
       Roll period: median of the last 9 full rolls (zero-crossings about
       the 60 s mean, 0.3 deg hysteresis); also on the OLED graph screen.
       Graph / Clino / G buttons in the title row (per phone) pick the view:
       the graph, the Clinometer (with Reset drag pointers, shown as large
       as the roll; one pair of arrows sets the scale, the same both sides,
       10-60 deg in 5: true angles from 40 up, stretched under 40 to zoom in;
       the pivot stays at the top) or the G-meter (with Reset peaks and the
       "max" line; the bottom arrows set the circle's full scale, left, and
       the vertical bar's span, right, in 0.1 g; arrows with values mark the
       highest and lowest vertical g on the bar) - the same as OLED screens 6
       and 10, from the live data; arrows a view doesn't use show -. The
       view scales are kept per phone. Bottom arrows: > makes the number
       bigger (the limit further down).
    3  OLED display: screen (all 10), brightness (the knob or the slider: the
       "Brightness from the knob" checkbox, EEPROM), On / Off, Flip (rotate 180 deg).
    4  Weather (appears when the BMP388 or SHT30 is switched on in card 16):
       pressure, inHg, sea-level pressure from keel to bridge - the draft (quarter mean),
       3-h change, air temp, humidity, dew point; live graph of one at a time
       over 5 / 20 min, 1 / 3 h from GET /wx, air temp with the dew point.
    5  Maxima, roll statistics and GM (behind the "Show Maxima, Roll
       Statistics and GM" button; remembered per phone); the GM estimate is described under card 1. Maxima: last 1 minute
       and all-time (the reset is in card 18). Roll statistics, last 10 or 30 min: median roll period, significant roll (mean of the
       highest third of roll amplitudes, like significant wave height),
       largest roll, RMS roll about the mean, mean list, rolls counted,
       and rolls whose peak went beyond a set angle to starboard / port.
       A roll = one full cycle accepted by the roll-period detector; its
       amplitude is half its peak-to-peak swing about the running mean.
    6  Waves (behind the "Show Waves" button below card 5;
       remembered per phone): from 2-Hz samples of the vertical
       acceleration (earth frame), roll and pitch over the last 17 min
       (first estimate after 8.5 min; Welch spectra every 30 s): the
       encounter period (pitch spectrum peak - the pitching period, as IMO
       MSC.1/Circ.1228 1.6 allows), the roll period Tr = the roll spectrum
       peak (natural roll, as the GM uses; v7.31 - before, card 1's median of
       the rolls, which forced rolling pulls towards Te), Te / Tr flagged within
       +/-10 % of 1 (synchronous / parametric) and of 0.5 (parametric) per
       Circ.1228 4.2.3 (the circular gives no tolerance), a heave Hs (a
       guide only: at the VMR's position, and the hull filters short
       waves) and a graph of the three spectra against period. Save image
       writes the whole card as a PNG (no preview). Off while At the dock:
       the Dock button's state is kept in the VMR (EEPROM; every phone
       follows it) and AT DOCK / UNDERWAY are marked in the CSV.
    7  Recording: START / STOP LOGGING. Nothing is written until Start,
       except the last 20 min (v8.62): while not recording, the rows go to
       1-min files in a hidden /.buf folder (the newest 21 kept); Save Last
       20min (top right, off while recording) joins them under a header into
       <start>_last20min.csv in the top folder.
       Logging rate (EEPROM; not while recording): 10 Hz, or with the BNO085
       off in card 16 also 1 s, 5 s, 20 s, 1 min, 5 min or 10 min - rows on
       the clock (e.g. 1-min rows at :00 s), the first at Start, plus a row at
       each event (Mark event, location mark, pause / resume ...). At 1 min and
       slower, Datalogger mode sleeps between rows (see LOGGER).
       START asks for an optional name (added to the file name) and the
       loading condition (written in the file header), and one file for
       the whole recording or a new file each day at 00:00 UTC (named for
       that day, its header says which file it continues; remembered per
       phone); every Start opens a new file. While recording, "Mark event" (optional note) tags the
       next row in the event / event_note columns.
    8  Location marks (behind "Show Location Marks", just below
       card 7; per phone): MARK records the mark number, the time
       (shown in LT; the file has LT, ZD and UTC), a Note filled in later on the
       row, lat / lon (from the GPS text, blank without a fix; shown in card 14's
       format, the file has dd mm.mmmm and decimal degrees), COG / SOG (card 1's
       averages), the add-on pressure, air temperature and humidity (blank when
       off); newest on top. Start cast / Stop cast
       make one row with the end time and position, the duration and the
       drift (great circle, start to end); one cast at a time. PCF8574 pins
       (card 17): the Location mark button makes a MARK; holding the Cast
       button while pressing it starts a cast, or stops the open one. The
       OLED shows MARK / CAST START / CAST STOP for 2.5 s. In datalogger mode
       the Mark button makes no mark: it wakes the VMR (from sleep only with
       the PCF8574 INT pin wired to A4) and leaves datalogger mode, like B.
       While recording, each is an event (LOCATION MARK n, CAST n START / STOP).
       The file also has GPS satellites, HDOP and fix age. Delete last
       (confirm) removes the newest row. Files: card 9, Marks tab. Saved silently at each
       mark and note to /location_mark/<first mark's LT>LT_location_marks.csv
       (plus decimal lat_deg / lon_deg); the open list comes back after a
       restart. Save to Phone (the CSV), Copy (the CSV text), Delete last, New file
       (confirm, naming the file kept: a new list from mark 1 and a new file at
       the next mark; the old file stays on the card; named Clear in v6.2-7.11),
       Clear (red, confirm: deletes the list and its file from the card).
    9  microSD files: file manager (see below).
    10 Plot from file: pick a CSV and a span (last 1-48 h or whole file),
       tap the items to plot (Roll, Pitch, Battery V, SOG, Roll period,
       GM, Trim, List - those two from calibrated files, v2.7 on -
       Pressure, Air temp (deg F), Humidity - from the add-ons, blank in
       the CSV while they are off or not answering;
       "5-min mean" adds a 5-minute average line to every panel;
       remembered per phone), then Make plot. A task at idle priority
       reads the file (sampling and logging carry on) and sends 600
       columns (min / max / mean for roll and pitch, means for the rest),
       the mean of every value over the whole span (in each panel title
       once there is a minute of data: list for roll, trim for pitch) and
       the event markers. The phone draws a PNG in the page's theme
       colours, with local time from the file's ZD and each axis rounded
       out to whole units of its data (degrees, knots, s; 0.1 V and
       0.1 m). Roll period and GM also get a dashed line at that
       whole-span mean. With the loading computer's GM in the file header
       (card 15), the GM panel also gets it as a solid line and a box
       comparing every row's GM with it: the VMR's mean, SD and range, the
       bias (VMR - loading computer, ft and %), the RMS difference and the
       share of the time within +/-10 %. Save to System puts the image in /plots on the
       microSD card (card 9, Plots tab); Save to Phone downloads it.
       Other plots (v8.62, behind the card's Other plots button):
       GM plot (v8.61): the whole file's logged GM (gm_ft) over time against
       a CargoMax GM - the one typed in when asked, or blank for the file
       header's - dashed, +/-10 % shaded, with the median, mean, range and
       share of rows within +/-10 % (from a 0.02-ft histogram of every row); the axis fits
       the plotted GM +/-0.2 ft. Waves (the spectra now), Weather (pressure,
       air temp + dew point, humidity) and Wind rose (live, over the Live span
       picked) go to the same preview and Save buttons.
       Cancel stops a plot being made (the task stops at its next 8 KB
       chunk). Untested estimate: 1-3 min for 24 h at 10 Hz.
    11 At the dock (the Dock button top right; kept in the
       VMR, so every phone follows it, and it switches the wave analysis
       off; shown at the very top in place of card 1; also OLED screen 9): trim in feet = L x
       tan(pitch), by the stern / head, and list to 0.01 deg, both means of
       1 s means over the window set on the card (30 s, 1, 2 or 5 min; a
       countdown while it fills), plus the live values and the draft difference
       across the beam that the list gives. L = LBP, or LOA.
       Calibrate: type the forward and aft drafts read at the marks (and
       midships, optional) as feet-inches (33-04); the VMR stores the
       pitch offset that makes the window's mean equal that trim
       (EEPROM). Without it the trim follows level zero. Midships shows
       the hog / sag and quarter-mean draft at the time. The sensor reads
       the deck slope where it is mounted and hull bending changes that
       slope near the ends: 0.1 deg is about 1.6 ft on 280 m, so
       recalibrate when the loading changes much.
    Developer settings (button below card 10; remembered per phone). The
    button shows a warning count if the hardware check finds anything.
    12 Hardware check (red when something is wrong): a crash / watchdog /
       brownout restart (where the loop was, core-dump backtrace; Sketch >
       Export Compiled Binary gives the .elf for decoding), I2C parts not
       answering, IMU recoveries (see below). Copy puts the text on the
       clipboard.
    13 Serial monitor (behind "Show Serial Monitor"; per phone; GET /serial
       only while it is shown): the last 10 lines the VMR printed (the same text as
       the USB Serial Monitor, 115200 baud; the VMR keeps the last 30).
       Expand (per phone) makes it 3x taller: all 30. Copy puts the lines on the clipboard.
       No wrapping: the card scrolls sideways. "Show on OLED" (latching)
       puts the last 10 on the OLED in a small font, one line each (cut
       at 32 characters), newest at the bottom, even if the OLED
       is switched off, until tapped again;
       the OLED then goes back to its screen. Not kept across a restart.
    14 GPS settings and diagnosis (behind "Show GPS Settings and Diagnosis"; per phone).
       Lat / lon format for this phone's dashboard: dd.ddd deg, dd mm.mmm
       (default) or dd mm ss - card 1, the location marks table (one more decimal)
       and the Waves image; the OLED and the files keep their own formats.
       Then: module talking (NMEA sentences per second) or silent, fix type,
       the instantaneous SOG (kn and m/s), COG and altitude (or why there is no fix), satellites
       used / in view (GPS and GLONASS), strongest signal (dB-Hz), antenna
       in use (internal / external active / fault), HDOP, plus Restart GPS
       (re-send set-up), Cold start (forget saved satellite data,
       search from scratch) and Turn off / on (standby; GPS columns blank;
       not kept across a restart). "Show sky plot" opens the Sky plot: every
       satellite in view (the list is only sent while it is shown).
       Also the SOG / COG average (1-60 s, default 10).
    15 Level zero and ship data (v8.4: level zero, card 16 before, at the top; behind "Show Level Zero
       and Ship Data"). Level zero: Set / Clear (same as holding A+C on screen 3), plus arrows that
       shift the roll or pitch reading 0.1 deg per tap. Set makes roll read 0 (upright) and pitch 0, or,
       with the fwd and aft drafts entered, the deck slope they give at the VMR: the trim plus the hog /
       sag slope at its distance from midships (levelRefPitch). Ship data (EEPROM: kept through restarts and firmware updates), in
       two parts: "This voyage" (drafts, displacement, TPI, Cb, Cwp,
       parallel WL, the loading computer's GM (ft, optional: written to the
       CSV header for Plot from file's comparison) - they change with the
       loading) and "The ship" (beam,
       LOA, LBP, keel heights - fixed). One Save for both. Fields:
       drafts fwd, mid (optional) and aft on the top row in feet-inches
       (33-04; the quarter mean (F + 6M + A) / 8 is the draft for GM, sea-level pressure and the
       rest - the mean of fwd and aft without a mid draft (v8.12); mid also shows the hog / sag; separate from the dock
       calibration), then in metres: beam, LOA, LBP (optional; the length
       for GM and the dock trim, LOA if blank), parallel WL (optional,
       kept for reference), keel to main deck, keel to bridge (bridge
       deck / wings) and keel to very top; where the VMR is (v8.0): from
       midships (+ forward / aft), from the centreline (+ starboard / port)
       and above the keel, for moving its heave to midships; then the hull form: block
       coefficient, waterplane coefficient, TPI (long tons / inch) and the
       transit displacement from the loading computer, with a unit
       picker (LT / MT; stored as LT);
       displacement, not deadweight; 0 or
       blank = not set. Shows the Cb the displacement implies (or the
       displacement from Cb) and the Cwp the TPI implies, and
       the draft (quarter mean), hog / sag, freeboard and air draft at that draft, and the GM working (now, T, C); the help text is behind
       "Show instructions" (as in most cards).
    16 I2C add-ons (behind "Show I2C Add-ons"; per phone; EEPROM): the BNO085 IMU
       can be switched off (v7.0; no motion data then: card 1 says so, the IMU
       columns are blank, no IMU warnings, and the slower logging rates of card 7
       become available; switching it on sets the rate back to 10 Hz, with a
       pop-up; neither while recording); then switch on each plug-in STEMMA QT part and pick
       its address - BMP388 (0x77/0x76), SHT30 (0x44/0x45), PCF8574
       (0x20-0x27), one ADS7830 (0x48/0x49/0x4B; 0x4A is the IMU). What
       their pins do is card 17's. Status per part, Scan (every address
       that answers). The BMP388 / SHT30 bring card 4 (Weather). Started at
       boot (boot-list lines) and retried every 5 s; a PCF8574 that stops
       answering leaves the OLED as it was. Saved on its own (v8.1: a Save
       here leaves card 17's settings as they are).
    17 Pin mappings (v8.1; EEPROM): which pin each control (pot, pot switch,
       screen, Mark, Cast) and LED (Mark, Recording, Fault) uses, the LEDs'
       state, Test LEDs, and the ADC channels and GPIO pins live.
    18 System: last restart and version; Wi-Fi health (this phone's signal
       at the VMR, phones connected, this page's own update timing - no
       extra traffic); Devices: the last 4 to join the hotspot, newest first,
       joined (LT) and on now / left, named by each phone's "This device's
       name" box (kept on that phone; else its type), from GET /devices every
       10 s while the developer settings are open; memory and web timing; Copy (the whole card as
       text); then four buttons: Restart VMR (closes the log file first),
       Restart Wi-Fi (drops every phone a few seconds; frees the places of
       phones that left without disconnecting - the hotspot takes 4),
       Reset I2C bus, Reset maxima.
    19 Firmware update over Wi-Fi, behind a password (v8.0, checked by the VMR):
       upload the compiled app image
       (Sketch > Export Compiled Binary, VMR.ino.bin). The VMR checks it is
       an ESP32-S2 app, writes it to the spare app slot, verifies it and
       restarts into it; anything wrong and the old firmware keeps running.
       Logging stops when the upload starts and the new firmware starts
       fresh, not recording. Settings, level zero and maxima are kept.
       The OLED shows the update even if it is switched off (full
       contrast): percent, "Vessel Measurement Recorder", old --> new version
       (the new one read from the FW_MARK text in the uploaded file, about
       15 % in; "..." until then, "?" for a build without it), kB received and a progress bar.
       Needs a partition scheme with OTA, flashed once over USB: Tools >
       Partition Scheme > "Minimal SPIFFS (1.9MB APP with OTA/128KB
       SPIFFS)". The default TinyUF2 No OTA scheme has no spare slot (the
       card says so).
    20 Cards on this phone (v8.0): hide cards and drag them into the order
       wanted, per phone. Only the page changes; Paused and At the dock always
       show when they apply.

    IMU NOT FOUND (OLED): the IMU did not start at boot, even after the
      VMR power-cycled it once. Shown over every screen (even with the OLED
      switched off) until it starts - the VMR keeps trying every 5 s - and
      asks for a full power-cycle (USB and battery out for 10 s).
    IMU going quiet while running (v3.7): the reports are asked for again
      every 5 s; only after 1 min of silence is the STEMMA QT rail
      power-cycled (also restarting the OLED and add-ons), then at most
      every 1, 2 min. A command the BNO085 cannot take is abandoned after
      0.25 s instead of hanging the VMR (v3.4). Counts in card 12.
      Every power-cycle of the rail (boot, recovery, Reset I2C bus) holds
      it off 1 s with SDA and SCL driven low, so nothing can keep the
      BNO085 half-powered through its bus pins (v3.8: before, a software
      restart such as an OTA update could leave it dead until the whole
      VMR was power-cycled).
    PAUSE (top right): for moving the VMR. Live values keep updating, but
      no rows are logged and nothing is counted: maxima, the 1-min window,
      roll period and statistics all hold; the waves analysis (and so GM)
      starts again, 8.5 min after Resume. While
      recording, an event row "PAUSED" is written as it stops and
      "RESUMED" when rows start again, so the gap shows in the file and on
      plots. The OLED shows a full-screen PAUSED notice (even if switched
      off). Resume here or with OLED button B. Not kept across a restart. If it is mounted differently after
      the move, set Level zero again (works while paused) before Resume.

    CHARGE (top right, confirmed first): everything that can be is switched
      off and the ESP32 goes into deep sleep, to charge as fast as possible:
      logging stops (file closed), settings saved, GPS to standby, OLED off,
      the STEMMA QT rail off (IMU, EEPROM, add-ons and the I2C pull-ups, so the
      battery gauge too) with the bus lines held low, Wi-Fi off. The Feather's
      charge LED goes out when the battery is full. OLED button B or the
      screen button (Feather pin 12) wakes it, as does turning it off and on:
      a fresh boot, "Last reset: deep sleep wake".
    LOGGER (top right): datalogger mode, only while recording (it never
      starts a recording itself). Recording continues; Wi-Fi and the
      OLED switch off and the CPU slows. The red LED blips every 5 s.
      At a logging rate of 1 min or slower (IMU off) it also sleeps between
      rows (light sleep: memory, the open file and the clock kept; GPS in
      standby), waking 20 s before each row for a fresh fix and add-on
      readings; the OLED stays off. The screen button shows the countdown
      to the next row on the OLED for 10 s (proof of life), then it goes
      off again. Buttons on the PCF8574 wake it from sleep only with the
      PCF8574's INT pin wired to Feather A4 (any input change wakes it for
      a moment); without that wire they work only while it is awake. No
      marks are made in Datalogger mode: the Mark button, like OLED button
      B, leaves it. Press OLED button B or the Mark button
      to leave Datalogger mode (Wi-Fi and the OLED back). Not kept across a
      restart. Before each sleep the file is closed and reopened, so
      cutting the power while it sleeps loses no rows. After a crash (panic
      or watchdog) the recording resumes in the same file, with a CRASH
      RESTART event (and Datalogger mode, if it was on); after a power loss
      or other restart it does not (press Start again: a new file).
    The hotspot has no internet; if your phone offers to "use cellular
    instead", decline / choose "stay connected".

  MICROSD FILE MANAGER (dashboard)
    Downloads and deletes are only available while logging is STOPPED
    (enforced by the firmware, not just the page).
    Lists the files on the card. Tap the arrow to download one file;
    select several (or "Download all") to get them as one .zip
    (uncompressed). Delete selected / Delete all are confirmed first.
    Archive selected / Archive all move those files into the folder
    /archive (made the first time; a name already there gets _2, _3 ...). The Archive tab
    lists that folder with the same download / zip / delete buttons, plus
    "Move selected back to the card". The Plots tab lists /plots: the
    images saved with Save to System (card 10), to view (View: a pop-up,
    also while recording), download or delete. The
    Marks tab lists /location_mark: one CSV per location marks list.
    Plot from file uses the top folder only (move a file back to plot it).
    Downloads land in Downloads (Android) or Files > Downloads (iOS);
    tap the .zip there to unpack it.
    - The live dashboard numbers pause while a download is running.
    - Speed is limited by the ESP32 Wi-Fi hotspot (untested estimate:
      well under 1 MB/s). One day at 10 Hz is roughly 80 MB, so whole
      days take minutes each. For weeks of data, pull the card.
    - One .zip is limited to 4 GB and ZIP_MAX_FILES files.

  IMU ON I2C (BNO085 via STEMMA QT)
    The BNO085 stretches the I2C clock; Adafruit lists it as not working
    well with ESP32 parts, and it can drop out. Its driver also retries a
    failed write forever, so the firmware only sends it commands when it
    answers on I2C.
    Every report is read through imuEventHandler(): the library's
    getSensorEvent() keeps only the last report of each transfer, which
    lost the orientation whenever the gyro report shared its transfer
    and caused false "silent IMU" power-cycles while data was flowing.
    If no orientation arrives for IMU_SILENT_MS while the loop is polling
    (time the loop spends elsewhere, e.g. on the SD card, doesn't count):
      1. if the BNO085 answers on I2C, the reports are requested again;
      2. if it is still silent, or not answering, the STEMMA QT rail is
         power-cycled and the bus cleared. A power-cycle that brings no
         data back doubles the wait before the next (10 s ... 2 min).
    Both are counted in the Hardware check card. System card: "Reset I2C
    bus" does step 2 by hand. If the driver still hangs, the main-loop
    watchdog restarts the VMR (recording stops; press Start again).

  SIGN CONVENTION / MOUNTING
    Mount the BNO085 flat, chip side UP, X arrow toward the bow. Y then
    points to PORT (right-handed axes). Roll is about the fore-aft axis,
    pitch about the athwartships axis.
    Roll is shown as S (starboard side down) or P (port side down).
    In the SD log roll_deg is signed: + = starboard down, - = port down.
    Pitch: + = bow up, - = bow down.
    Yaw increases, and rate of turn is +, when turning to STARBOARD
    (clockwise seen from above), like a ship's heading.
    Check once: starboard down must read S, bow up must read +, a turn to
    starboard must read ROT +. If any is backwards, set ROLL_FLIP /
    PITCH_FLIP / YAW_FLIP to 1.
    Yaw uses the Game Rotation Vector by default (gyro + accel, no
    magnetometer), so it is a RELATIVE heading that drifts slowly - not a
    compass heading. On a steel hull the magnetometer is unreliable anyway.
    Magnetometer TEST (v8.0): the calibrated field is read at 10 Hz on
    its own and shown under Yaw in card 1 - a tilt-corrected magnetic
    heading (the ship's deviation NOT removed), the field strength, its dip
    and the BNO085's calibration status, also in the CSV (mag_heading_deg,
    mag_field_ut, mag_cal). It does not feed roll, pitch or yaw.
    Yaw rate is shown and logged as rate of turn in deg/min. Its maximum
    is peak |rate of turn|, since a maximum of a 0-360 heading has no
    meaning.

  SD LOG
    Written only between START and STOP on the dashboard. Every Start
    opens a new file named by the UTC time START was tapped in the name
    box, plus the name typed there (letters, digits, - and _, up to 23):
    /YYYYMMDD_HHMMSSZ_name.csv, or /YYYYMMDD_HHMMSSZ.csv with no name
    (no valid clock yet: /NOCLK_001_name.csv ...). If power is lost while
    recording, it is NOT resumed: only START LOGGING starts a recording
    (or creates a file). The dashboard says the last recording was cut
    off. Times in the log are UTC.
    Rows wait in a 256 KB PSRAM buffer (about 3 min at 10 Hz) while the
    card is busy or failing; a failed card is remounted after 2 s, then
    4, 8, 10 s ... and the backlog is written 8 KB at a time. The first row
    after an outage is an event "SD RECOVERED after N s (M rows lost)";
    rows are lost only if the buffer fills (v7.34; before, every row
    during an outage was lost and the remount was tried every 10 s).
    The file starts with metadata lines beginning with # (system, firmware
    build, credits, device id, file name, name suffix, loading condition, start
    time and time source, ZD, log / GPS / IMU rates, axis conventions,
    level-zero offsets, every ship data item (ship_*_m; blank = not
    entered), the dock trim calibration, notes on the derived columns).
    Read it with pandas as pd.read_csv(path, comment='#').
    Columns: utc_time, millis, roll_deg, pitch_deg, yaw_rate_dpm (v8.5: yaw_deg gone),
             gps_lat, gps_lon, gps_alt_m, gps_speed_ms, gps_speed_kn,
             gps_angle_deg, gps_fix, gps_sats, gps_hdop, gps_fix_quality,
             gps_age_ms, batt_v, batt_pct, zero_applied, roll_period_s,
             gm_ft, event, event_note, acc_fwd_g, acc_stbd_g, acc_up_g,
             pressure_hpa, air_temp_c, rh_pct (I2C add-ons; blank when off),
             trim_ft, list_deg (blank until the trim is calibrated),
             wave_te_s, wave_tr_s, wave_tr_src, wave_te_tr, wave_flag,
             heave_hs_m, heave_tp_s, roll_tp_s, heave_hs_mid_m (the Waves card's
             estimates, only on the row after each new one, every 30 s; the
             last: heave Hs moved to midships, blank without the VMR's position),
             mag_heading_deg, mag_field_ut, mag_cal (magnetometer test,
             v8.0: magnetic heading, deviation not removed; field; the
             BNO085's calibration status 0-3), wind_app_deg, wind_app_kn,
             wind_true_deg, wind_true_kn (v8.2, wind meter, 1-s means once
             a second; true wind estimated with COG as the heading)
    Slow columns (v8.2): batt_v, batt_pct, zero_applied, roll_period_s,
    gm_ft, the add-on weather and mag_cal are written together once a
    second and when one changes; blank between (= unchanged).
    trim_ft / list_deg: the dock card's trim and list, instantaneous (the
    card shows means over its 30 s - 5 min window). A calibration, clearing it or setting / clearing
    level zero while recording is marked in event_note (TRIM CALIBRATED,
    LEVEL ZERO SET ...); the arrow nudges of level zero are not.
    acc_*: the BNO085 accelerometer (gravity included, i.e. specific
    force, what is felt) in ship axes, in g, the mean of the ~5 samples in
    each row; level and still = 0, 0, +1. Heeled to starboard acc_stbd_g
    is negative (the deck pushes up-slope); level-zero tilt is removed
    when it is on. Subtract gravity using roll / pitch for motion only.
    They come after event_note so older files keep the same column
    numbers.
    roll_period_s and gm_ft are blank until a period is measured (gm_ft also
    when the beam, a draft or the LOA is not entered); event / event_note are blank
    except on the row a marker was written to. Event notes are plain ASCII
    up to 40 characters (commas, quotes and # are replaced by spaces).
    Rows are written at LOG_INTERVAL_MS (10 Hz) for the IMU, or at the
    slower rate chosen in card 7 when the IMU is off (each row then holds the
    readings of that moment, not averages). Without IMU data the roll, pitch,
    yaw and ROT columns are blank (they read "nan" before v7.0). The GPS
    columns are filled only on the first row after a new GPS sentence
    (once per fix interval) and left blank on the rows in between, so no
    GPS value is ever duplicated. A fix-lost report is logged too
    (gps_fix = 0, sats filled, position blank). gps_age_ms is the delay
    from the fix arriving to the row being written (< 100 ms).

  GPS RATE (GPS_RATE_HZ)
    1, 5 or 10 Hz. A higher rate gives more fixes per second, not more
    accurate fixes. Per the PA1616D datasheet: 3.0 m CEP / 0.1 m/s
    unaided, 2.5 m / 0.05 m/s with SBAS - and SBAS only runs at <= 5 Hz.
    So 10 Hz switches SBAS off and is slightly LESS accurate per fix.
    1 Hz (default): ship position, SOG and COG change slowly, the motion
    data comes from the IMU, and 1 Hz runs at the GPS's stock 9600 baud
    with SBAS on. Above 1 Hz the GPS is switched to 115200 baud: RMC+GGA
    at 10 Hz is ~1.5 kB/s, more than 9600 baud carries.

  PROGRAM STRUCTURE
    loop() (the Arduino task) reads the GPS and IMU, keeps time, draws the
      OLED and writes the SD log. It is the only task that uses the I2C bus.
    webTask serves the dashboard. Changes asked for on the page are put in
      req* variables and applied by loop() in applyWebRequests(), so only
      one task ever touches I2C, the OLED or the settings image P. (A
      firmware update is written to flash by webTask as it arrives; loop()
      only draws its progress and restarts.) webTask also owns the location
      marks (RAM list and file); loop() only signals the PCF8574 buttons
      (reqMarkBtn) and draws the OLED lines webTask prepares (markOled).
    Log replaces Serial: everything printed also goes to a 30-line copy
      for the dashboard's serial monitor card and the OLED.
    plotTask is started by the "Plot from file" card and runs at idle
      priority. All SD access (loop, web, plot) goes through sdLock().
    Settings and all-time maxima live in P (struct Persist) and are saved
      to the EEPROM by servicePersist(); layouts V12-V14 are migrated on
      load, older ones start from the defaults (v7.4).
    The dashboard page and logo are kept readable in web/index.html and
      web/logo.svg (not compiled); web/make_page.py gzips them into the
      byte arrays at the end of this file. Edit them, run it, compile.
    crumb(ST_...) records what loop() is doing in RTC memory, so after a
      watchdog restart the dashboard can say where it was stuck.
    Comments like "---- ZIPCORE BEGIN / END ----" mark code that off-device
      tests extract and compile; keep them.

  SPEED UNITS
    The GPS reports speed over ground in knots. gpsSpeed is stored in m/s
    (knots x 0.514444). The SD log has both m/s and knots; screens show
    knots and m/s. COG (gpsAngle) is course over ground, not ship's head,
    and is meaningless when the ship is stopped.
  ============================================================================
*/

